in may 2009 7 astronauts flew to the  Hubble Space Telescope aboard space  shuttle Atlantis bringing to the ailing  observatory new batteries a new camera  and spare parts to fix broken hardware  it was the fifth servicing mission to  Hubble and the final planned repair  opportunity for the telescope the  astronauts brought more than replacement  parts with them however they also  carried an IMAX camera to space to  document their mission just a month  before Hubble's 20th birthday comes  hubble 3d a new documentary film  directed by Tony Myers here to discuss  the film is Tony Myers and the missions  commander Scott Altman thank you both  for joining us our pleasure so Tony what  about this mission appealed to you as a  filmmaker well first of all it I wanted  to make a film about Hubble that told  the life story of Hubble or as much of  it as we could do in our timeframe and  of course this was the final service  mission and I knew that there were a lot  of interesting spacewalks and really  challenging we like the most challenging  spacewalks ever to be performed we're  going to happen on it so I knew it was  going to be exciting good and in Scott  this is your fourth trip in space was  there anything different for you this  time in terms of feeling added pressure  because the scientific stakes were so  high and everyone was watching from the  ground well I think that's the key point  we knew on my last mission that there  were future servicing missions planned  and while there's always a challenge to  get stuff done you think well someone  else could finish if we had to on this  one we knew it was the final trip last  chance Hubble's been such an incredible  instrument we all wanted to do as much  as we could to put it in his greatest  shape as it could be for it to continue  its voyage of discovery Antonio if you  would talk about some of the challenges  that you faced as a filmmaker for whom  so many of the shots are out of your  control you're on the ground the  telescope is in the hands of the  astronauts I'm sorry that the cameras in  the hands with astronauts we do train  about eight months out from the flight  and we develop a scene list kind of wish  list of shots based on our observations  of there  for the space walks in the pool and the  challenges really are only the timeline  we of course there's a sunrise and  sunset every 90 minutes and we know that  if they get behind on this tightly  scripted script then our best shots may  wind up being in the dark where we can't  shoot but we also know that's a  possibility so we do contingency  planning in the list as well and no I  totally trust them we train them to be  cinematographers and directors and we  also say if an alien flies up and puts  his face in the window just because it's  not on the list don't not shoot it you  know it's your the directors and  ultimately the decisions are in your  hands I see and in terms of working in  3d how does that change that process of  shooting film is there an actual  technical difference well there is a  technical difference of a big one in the  case of the camera we flew in the cargo  bay which is the imax 3d cargo bay  camera that carries a mile of film it's  a single load you can't change it out in  the vacuum and that yields eight minutes  of 3d film that's because it's shooting  both the left eye and the right eye for  3d that are normally on two strips on  one piece of film and so that's not a  lot eight minutes you have to plan it  out very carefully all the footage in  the film that we see that's the the  payload Bay camera that's taken from  eight minutes of raw footage that's  correct Wow yeah yeah a lot of pressure  not to leave too much on the cutting  room yeah no no absolutely they did a  bad punt but a stellar job and Tony uh  in the film there's some very wonderful  three dimensional fly throughs if you  just talk a little bit about how those  were made and how much emphasis was put  on keeping those scientifically accurate  I wanted to do two things I wanted to  explore a phenomenon that was familiar  to most people in the night sky even if  they're only part time  you know stargazers and that turned out  to be the Orion Nebula because most  people are familiar with Orion's belt  has something so I didn't wanted to to  start there and that also turned out to  be a very huge data set of Hubble's it's  actually when you combine the three  datasets that we used it's a billion  pixels worth of information and and so  that was one we used you know one for  the approach and then another data set  in in the interior for the trapezium  region where all that little baby solar  systems are and a third for the  accretion disk and we combined all of  those with a software program called  werder virtual director that allows you  to plot a flight path through the data  but it's all real data there there isn't  any you know just straight phony CGI in  there and the other the other concluding  sequence is one that I wanted to do to  try and convey in the simplest way  possible the scale of the universe and  so we in that when we fly from the milky  way out past Andromeda and then into the  Virgo cluster each time we pass  something we're going from a dozen  galaxies to 2,000 galaxies to a billion  galaxies so now in the film partly  because of the scale it's very easy to I  mean you and your fellow astronauts are  up on the screen and huge proportions  and you look like people who are about  to do something very serious and  possibly dangerous and that's not a side  of space flight that we get to see very  often is that you are there are there a  lot of nervous before you launch well I  think everybody is focused that's what I  talked folks I want to let's put our  game face on we're going out to do  something we want to make sure we're all  ready to meet this challenge head-on  it's going to take a lot of  concentration and focus to get up there  to get to the telescope and then to do  the spacewalks there's also a time to  relax and sort of recharge your  batteries  but I think it's important that we were  all ready to go and we got there and as  a veteran of four space flights would  you like to see the space shuttles life  extended beyond its its current for I  suppose for more lot more flights right  now the shuttle is an amazing amazing  vehicle its capability to be reusable to  take so much payload to orbit and bring  it home something that doesn't exist  anywhere else on the planet right now  transitions are always hard when you  move from something you know to  something that you don't but they're  also important to make progress to add  capability and I'm hopeful that we can  make that transition safely and find a  new path both to low-earth orbit and  Beyond to go back to the moon and on to  Mars and if space tourism becomes  reality any time soon would you be  pitching to get back up there you know  John Grunsfeld says he's happier in  space than anywhere else and you can see  it in the movie with the big grin on his  face when he comes in from a spacewalk I  feel that way a lot myself to it is an  incredible adventure and I'd love to be  a part of it again maybe next time take  my wife and kids along me too please  yeah me too which is a great thing about  imax is because it gives you the chance  to share that experience with people who  can't go my family to be there sitting  in the movie theater with my wife and  son sitting next to me was really a  special experience for me last night  thank you very much oh great it's been a  cue it's been a pleasure talking to both  of you enjoyed his father
 

